## THE BLOCKER, in your own words from the pass-2 review

> *"There is no shared functioning detector here — the permitted probe detects **NEITHER** repair.
> The two expected-value helpers already exist, but they implement distinct instruction formulas."*

> *"Each commit should carry instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying
> mutants, including a separate width-sensitive observation for `rlwimi`."*

**That is vacuity-floor clause (a) unmet for two HIGH rounds: a detector must FAIL on the pre-fix
build, and nothing currently does.** Both fixes are written, measured correct by exhaustion, and
**cannot ship** until this is closed.

## SETTLED — do not re-derive

* **`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` is CORRECT.** Book I RTL `m <- MASK(b, ¬n)` with `n` six bits, so
  `¬n` is `63-SH`. Verified across **all 4,096 mask combinations**. The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh`
  *after* `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at `-1`.
* **`rlwimi`: correct across all 1,024 mask pairs.** The fix narrows the rotation source to
  `uint32_t` and adds the wrapping high-word insertion.
* **Book I `:578`** — `MASK(x,y)` wraps when `x > y`. **`:342`/`:1989`** — both execution modes
  affect all 64 bits when an instruction sets a 64-bit register; every GPR is 64 bits.
* **These are TWO commits, not one** — same semantic class, different fix designs.
* An open question, NOT this brief's subject: whether any implementation compiles the 32-bit
  `MODE_uint_t` variant where the wrapping high-half write is discarded.

## THE DIFF UNDER TEST

```c
X(rldimi)                                   /* the mask-termination fix */
-	int m;                                  int m, n = sh;
-	while (sh-- != 0) { ... }               while (n-- != 0) { ... }
 	m = mb; tmp = 0;
 	do { tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));
-		m ++;                               m = (m + 1) & 63;
-	} while (m != 63 - sh);                 } while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));

X(rlwimi)                                   /* the width + wrapping-mask fix */
-	MODE_uint_t tmp = reg(ic->arg[0]), ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);
+	uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(ic->arg[0]);
+	MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);
 	tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;
+	if (mb > me)                            /* a wrapping mask selects the high word */
+		ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);
```

## THE QUESTIONS

1. **Confirm the probe really detects neither**, from its source — which rows exist, what they
   assert, and why no combination of them varies under either fix. **If one row DOES discriminate,
   the blocker is wrong and that is the answer I want.**
2. **Design the `rldimi` rows.** What `(RS, SH, MB)` triples make the pre-fix
   `while (m != 63 - sh)` produce a *different* mask from `MASK(MB, 63-SH)`? **Give concrete
   operands and the expected RA both ways.** Include the boundary `mb == (64-sh)&63`, where my
   reading is that the loop must set all 64 bits.
3. **Design the `rlwimi` rows.** You called for *"a separate width-sensitive observation"* — what
   is it concretely? **Give operands where the pre-fix 64-bit rotation differs observably from the
   32-bit one**, and separately where a wrapping `(MB > ME)` mask must write the duplicated high
   word.
4. **THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT FOR EACH FIX** — the fix present, its repair nulled. For `rldimi` my
   candidate is reverting the loop termination while keeping `n = sh`; for `rlwimi`, keeping the
   `uint32_t tmp` and deleting the `if (mb > me)` block. **Name the better ones if these are weak,
   and for each say which of your proposed rows kills it.**
5. *** THE QUESTION THAT HAS CAUGHT THREE VACUOUS ROWS TODAY: for each row you propose, name the
   mutant it kills AND the observable that differs under that mutant. ***  A row whose channel
   cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous by construction and that is knowable now. **Today
   a sibling round found six mutants scoring a total escape because every row probed one position;
   another found a detector where a zero-expectation row could not discriminate because the
   defective path also produced zero.**
6. **One probe or two?** These are two commits. Does one probe with two row groups satisfy "same
   detector, no new oracle", or does each round owe its own file? **Note `ppc_rldicl_probe.py`
   already exists for a THIRD PowerPC round (`#522`), so say whether extending it is right or
   whether that couples three rounds' detectors together.**
7. **Is the probe gated?** Check `gate_ppc.sh`. **A detector in `regress/` that no gate executes is
   a defect this project has a standing row for**, so if the existing probe is ungated, say so and
   say what wiring the new rows owes.
8. **What can a probe actually OBSERVE here?** These are register-to-register instructions with no
   device side effects. Say plainly whether the rows must be an offline differential, a
   cold-debugger register readback on an unmodified in-tree machine, or something else — **and if a
   rung-3 witness is feasible, say which in-tree machine and how.**

